  "generated_text": "To solve the inequality \\(10 < -x + 13\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Isolate the variable term**:\n   \\[\n   10 < -x + 13\n   \\]\n   Subtract 13 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   10 - 13 < -x\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left side:\n   \\[\n   -3 < -x\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve for \\(x\\)**:\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-1\\). Remember that multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign:\n   \\[\n   3 > x\n   \\]\n   This can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   x < 3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify the positive integer solutions**:\n   The positive integer values of \\(x\\) that satisfy \\(x < 3\\) are 1, 2, and 3.\n\nTherefore, the number of positive integer values of \\(x\\) that are solutions to the inequality \\(10 < -x + 13\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[3, '3']",
    "gold_answer": "We first solve the inequality: \\begin{align*}\n10 & < -x + 13\\\\\n-3 & < -x\\\\\n3 & > x. \\end{align*} The only positive integers less than 3 are 1 and 2, for a total of $\\boxed{2}$ solutions.",
    "is_correct": false
